WEBVTT

00:00:00.181 --> 00:00:03.618
The surprise of an outcome with
probability p is minus log p.

00:00:03.618 --> 00:00:08.711
With probabilities one half, one quarter
and one quarter, the surprises are one,

00:00:08.712 --> 00:00:09.794
two and two bits.

00:00:09.793 --> 00:00:11.418
Entropy is their average:

00:00:11.418 --> 00:00:13.231
one point five bits.

00:00:13.231 --> 00:00:15.268
Now change the distribution.

00:00:15.268 --> 00:00:19.606
A certain outcome has entropy zero, and
the uniform one has the most:

00:00:19.606 --> 00:00:23.131
log K, which is exactly two bits for four
classes.

00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:26.472
Cross-entropy is the average surprise when
outcomes follow p,

00:00:26.473 --> 00:00:28.361
but you score them with a model q.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.085
It splits into two parts:

00:00:30.085 --> 00:00:33.310
the entropy of p, plus the KL divergence.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:38.060
As q moves towards p, KL shrinks to zero,
but entropy stays.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.185
It is a floor no model can beat.

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:45.060
So with a fixed target, minimizing
cross-entropy is the same as minimizing
KL.

00:00:45.208 --> 00:00:46.758
Is KL a distance?

00:00:46.758 --> 00:00:51.408
Take p as one half, one half, and q as
zero point eight, zero point two.

00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:54.846
Would KL of p from q equal KL of q from p?

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:56.358
Pause and decide.

00:00:57.877 --> 00:00:58.277
No:

00:00:58.277 --> 00:01:02.004
they are zero point two two three and zero
point one nine three,

00:01:02.005 --> 00:01:03.228
so direction matters.

00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:06.065
Softer targets are KL at work too.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:09.392
Label smoothing moves a little mass to the
wrong classes,

00:01:09.393 --> 00:01:14.178
which caps the best gap between logits at
log thirty-seven, about three point six.

00:01:14.177 --> 00:01:17.227
Distillation softens a teacher with a
temperature.

00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:21.502
At T equals four, its logits show how it
ranks the wrong classes.

00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:24.655
Contrastive learning is classification
over a batch.

00:01:24.655 --> 00:01:29.730
CLIP compares every image with every
caption, and the diagonal holds the true
pairs.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.306
Each row asks an image to pick its
caption,

00:01:32.307 --> 00:01:35.243
and each column asks a caption to pick its
image.

00:01:35.242 --> 00:01:39.067
The temperature matters because cosines
live between minus one and one:

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.560
this example costs zero point eight seven
at tau of one,

00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:44.868
and almost nothing at zero point one.

00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:48.167
And if the scores carry no information,
the loss is log N.

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.267
That's chance.
